  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday morning through next Saturday)\nIssued at 232 PM EDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nAs the long term period begins, the axis of a longwave trough will \nbe centered over the Atlantic coast, which will keep much of Georgia \nunderneath northwesterly upper level flow. Meanwhile, surface high \npressure will be sliding across the Midwest. The combination of \nthese factors will keep weather conditions dry and benign across the \nmajority of the forecast area for one more day on Tuesday. Low \ntemperatures will start the morning in the low to mid 60s across \nnorth Georgia (and even some upper 50s in the highest elevations) \nand in the upper 60s and low 70s in central Georgia. Highs will \nclimb into the low 90s in north Georgia and mid 90s in central \nGeorgia on Tuesday afternoon. Isolated thunderstorms will be \npossible in portions of south-central Georgia during the peak \nheating hours, while dry air will keep chances minimal across the \nremainder of the forecast area.\n\nAtmospheric moisture will gradually over during the middle part of \nthe week, and weak disturbances traversing the northwesterly flow \nwill provide opportunity for thunderstorms on Wednesday afternoon \nand evening. On Thursday, another trough moving across the Great \nLakes will advance a front towards far north Georgia. The front will \ngradually work its way southeast through the forecast area on \nThursday into Friday. Forcing ahead of the front and increase \nmoisture will lead to scattered to numerous thunderstorms during the \nlater part of the week.",
